```latex
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Column 1 & Column 2 \\ \hline
Row 1, Column 1 & \begin{tabular}{c}Row 1, Column 2\\Row 1, Column 2\\Row 1, Column 2\end{tabular} \\ \hline
Row 2, Column 1 & Row 2, Column 2 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a LaTeX table with a multi-line cell.}
\label{tab:example}
\end{table}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to create a new line within the cell `Row 1, Column 2`. This allows you to split the content into multiple lines, making the table more readable and easier to understand.

